#: ../../source/arduino_start/06_basic_further_syntax.rst:16
msgid "Sketch Writing Rule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/06_basic_further_syntax.rst:19
msgid "If you ask a friend to turn on the lights for you, you can say \"Turn on the lights.\", or \"Lights on, bro.\", you can use any tone of voice you want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/06_basic_further_syntax.rst:21
msgid "However, if you want the Arduino board to do something for you, you need to follow the Arduino program writing rules to type in the commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/06_basic_further_syntax.rst:23
msgid "This chapter contains the basic rules of the Arduino language and will help you understand how to translate natural language into code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/06_basic_further_syntax.rst:25
msgid "Of course, this is a process that takes time to get familiar with, and it is also the most error-prone part of the process for newbies, so if you make mistakes often, it's okay, just try a few more times."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/06_basic_further_syntax.rst:29
msgid "Semicolon ``;``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/06_basic_further_syntax.rst:31
msgid "Just like writing a letter, where you write a period at the end of each sentence as the end, the Arduino language requires you to use ``;`` to tell the board the end of the command."
msgstr ""

